No — runs-on: enterprise-runners is not an operating system. It's the label of a self-
hosted GitHub Actions runner pool, same pattern as docker-maven-21-arc you saw
earlier.

Breaking down what's here:

yaml

runs-on: enterprise-runners       # ← self-hosted runner group name, not an OS
if: github.event_name == 'repository_dispatch'   # ← only runs when triggered via API

What enterprise-runners likely means:

- A GitHub Enterprise-managed runner group — likely a fleet of self-hosted runners
  (VMs or K8s pods, similar to your ARC setup) that GitHub Enterprise Cloud/Server lets
  an org define as a shared pool across multiple repos
- The actual OS underneath (Linux/Windows/container image) is defined wherever that
  runner pool is provisioned — not visible in this YAML

  ------------

  Correct — self-hosted, not GitHub's own infrastructure.

Quick recap of the two runner types:

                        GitHub-hosted runners              Self-hosted runners
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Who provisions          GitHub                              Your org (Worldpay/GlobalPayments)
the machine

Where it runs            GitHub's own cloud infra            Your own servers/K8s cluster/VMs

Labels used               ubuntu-latest,                     Custom names you define —
                          windows-latest, macos-              enterprise-runners,
                          latest                              docker-maven-21-arc, etc.

Network access            Public internet only, no access    Can sit inside your VPC — direct
                          to your private VPC/AWS             access to private ECR, internal
                          resources by default                APIs, AWS resources without
                                                               exposing them publicly

Cost model                 Pay per GitHub Actions             You pay for your own compute
                          minutes                            (EC2/EKS), no per-minute GH
                                                               charge


Both examples you've seen so far are self-hosted:

- docker-maven-21-arc → self-hosted via ARC (Actions Runner Controller) on
  Kubernetes/EKS
- enterprise-runners → self-hosted, likely a different runner pool — could be VM-
  based, or a separate Enterprise-managed runner group (GitHub Enterprise Cloud has a
  feature called "Enterprise Runner Groups" for shared self-hosted fleets across an org)

